{
    "id": "inspect-an-answer",
    "question": "Can you walk me through how to trace why Footnote gave an answer? Please link to two relevant documentation pages.",
    "response": {
        "action": "message",
        "message": "Footnote is built around inspectability, so there are two natural places to look: provenance and observability.\n\nThe **Provenance** documentation describes Footnote's core feature: a recorded trail of why an answer was produced, including which sources were considered and used as evidence [1](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/2.3-provenance). To inspect a specific answer, follow that trail: the response itself carries its evidence items and citations, and the provenance record shows the flow from prompt → retrieval → reasoning → response.\n\nFor a more operational view, the **Observability** documentation covers structured logging, lifecycle events, logging hygiene, and cost tracking [2](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/2.5-observability). That's where you'd find request IDs, timestamps, which model and capability profile were selected, token usage, and cost — useful when auditing why a particular run behaved the way it did.\n\nSo in practice: inspect the provenance trail for the *why* (inputs, sources, reasoning), and the observability logs for the *how it ran* (request lifecycle, model, tokens) [1](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/2.3-provenance), [2](https://deepwiki.com/footnote-ai/footnote/2.5-observability).\n\nOne caveat: these two links come from a current web search of the Footnote docs, and I haven't verified every field name or command on those pages line by line — the exact trace/log schemas live in the linked documentation.",
        "modality": "text",
